  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The evaluation relies on simulated index improvements rather than empirical, post-implementation measurements of temperature reduction or ecological health.",
      "what": "This study models the ecological and thermal impacts of Tehran's Green Space Master Plan (GSMP) using Biotope Area Factor (BAF) and Urban Cooling Capacity (UCC) indices. The proposed plan increases the city's overall BAF from 0.11 to 0.20 and its UCC from 0.146 to 0.211, though Gini coefficient analyses reveal these benefits disproportionately favor affluent northern districts. To address this spatial inequity, a modified scenario integrating green roofs in dense, disadvantaged neighborhoods was simulated, raising their local BAF from 0.06 to 0.35 and UCC from 0.18 to 0.35.",
      "why": "It quantifies the spatial inequities inherent in a major city's official greening plan and demonstrates how targeted vertical greening interventions can retroactively balance ecological benefits in dense, low-income areas."
    }
  },
